WEBVTT

01:30.230 --> 01:35.240
Light and Darkness

01:32.480 --> 01:35.410
We will now hold the fourth vote.

01:36.530 --> 01:38.850
I felt a weight hanging over me.

01:39.600 --> 01:41.630
The fight was over.

01:42.690 --> 01:44.190
I would lose.

01:45.190 --> 01:46.410
It was unavoidable.

01:48.660 --> 01:51.000
Let us tally the votes.

01:50.880 --> 01:54.360
Yuji Fukunaga



Tetsuzo Sato



 Takayoshi Miura


 Shingo Fujita


Hiroto Kitamura


Kosuke Tsunoda


 Teruyuki Eda


 Takahiro Kikuzawa


 Nao Kanzaki

01:51.380 --> 01:53.880
Fourth Vote

01:53.900 --> 01:56.380
\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\hTetsuzo Sato


\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\hTakayoshi Miura


\h\h\h\h\h\hShingo Fujita


 \h\h\h\h\hHiroto Kitamura 


\h\h\h\hKosuke Tsunoda


\h\h\hTeruyuki Eda



\hTakahiro Kikuzawa


 Nao Kanzaki

01:54.380 --> 01:56.380
Fifth Vote

02:05.150 --> 02:06.900
You look pathetic, Nao.

02:07.320 --> 02:09.890
Your loss is now an inevitability.

02:10.160 --> 02:12.410
You don't have to rub it in.

02:12.410 --> 02:15.140
But all this sitting and waiting 
is boring, don't you think?

02:17.010 --> 02:18.290
Well?

02:18.660 --> 02:21.420
How about going one-on-one with me?

02:21.780 --> 02:27.380
If you win, I'll give you 
all thirty of my votes.

02:27.380 --> 02:30.910
You'll give me your votes? 
Is that possible?

02:30.910 --> 02:33.710
Votes belong to the player
 who's earned them.

02:33.710 --> 02:35.790
They can be freely bought and sold...

02:36.140 --> 02:38.910
using these M Tickets.

02:39.890 --> 02:41.190
Yes, it's possible.

02:41.190 --> 02:44.810
If both parties agree, we'll change 
the vote totals on the board

02:44.810 --> 02:47.170
at the next tally announcement.

02:47.170 --> 02:48.270
You see?

02:49.350 --> 02:51.600
But what if I lose?

02:51.600 --> 02:55.460
You'll pay me 30 million yen
 with your M Tickets.

02:56.070 --> 02:57.600
That's so much.

02:57.600 --> 03:02.280
But I'll still be stuck at zero votes
if I don't at least try...

03:02.280 --> 03:04.040
Well? Up for it?

03:04.580 --> 03:07.080
What exactly do you want to play?

03:07.080 --> 03:09.230
The game couldn't be more simple.

03:10.560 --> 03:11.770
Playing cards?

03:12.510 --> 03:15.490
I brought them to kill time.

03:15.490 --> 03:18.430
But my deck has an unusual feature.

03:20.470 --> 03:21.810
A double-backed card!

03:21.810 --> 03:24.780
It's a misprint that got 
mixed in with the others.

03:24.780 --> 03:28.680
My game uses two cards: 
the double-backed one and a joker.

03:29.360 --> 03:31.310
Incidentally...

03:31.310 --> 03:34.860
Which do you prefer, light or darkness?

03:34.860 --> 03:38.830
Well... light, I suppose.

03:38.830 --> 03:40.090
Oh?

03:40.090 --> 03:41.650
The game is simple.

03:41.650 --> 03:44.580
I put the two cards in this bag
 where they can't be seen

03:44.580 --> 03:46.080
and mix them up.

03:46.360 --> 03:49.120
Then you draw one of them.

03:49.120 --> 03:52.920
When you do, make sure 
we can't see its face side.

03:52.920 --> 03:53.820
Right.

03:54.210 --> 03:56.780
What we see here is the back of the card,

03:56.780 --> 04:00.580
and its face side could be the joker 
or a copy of this design.

04:00.580 --> 04:03.220
There's a fifty-fifty chance of either.

04:03.220 --> 04:05.770
If the card comes up as 
your preferred "light" side,

04:05.770 --> 04:09.600
the card's face side featuring
 the joker, then you win.

04:09.600 --> 04:13.370
If it comes up the "dark" side, 
the reverse design, then I win.

04:13.370 --> 04:15.960
If the card is face-up when 
you draw it, it won't be fair,

04:15.960 --> 04:18.230
so we'll return it to 
the bag and try again.

04:18.230 --> 04:21.630
First to ten wins takes the game.

04:21.630 --> 04:22.830
Simple, no?

04:23.520 --> 04:26.380
There's no way to cheat you in this game.

04:26.380 --> 04:29.660
I mix up the cards. You draw them.

04:29.660 --> 04:31.490
It's unimpeachably fair.

04:31.850 --> 04:34.240
Well? Up for it?

04:36.170 --> 04:37.380
Let's do it.

04:38.570 --> 04:40.950
If I were to win, I'd get thirty votes.

04:40.950 --> 04:43.510
If Fukunaga-san did, 
I'd give her 30 million yen.

04:43.820 --> 04:47.720
We drew up the contract on M Tickets,
 and the game began.

04:52.780 --> 04:54.860
Go on. Flip it over.

04:57.230 --> 04:59.160
It's the face side! Yes!

04:59.160 --> 05:00.350
I won!

05:00.350 --> 05:04.360
Goodness. You're going to take
 my thirty votes, you little thief.

05:04.360 --> 05:06.610
Nao

05:04.360 --> 05:06.610
Fukunaga

05:04.880 --> 05:06.610
|

05:14.000 --> 05:17.870
Well, it's a fifty-fifty game, 
so it's bound to happen.

05:17.870 --> 05:19.380
Nao

05:17.870 --> 05:19.380
Fukunaga

05:17.870 --> 05:19.380
|

05:18.400 --> 05:19.380
|

05:19.770 --> 05:23.210
The game continued swiftly on.

05:25.380 --> 05:27.010
And in the end...

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
Nao

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
Fukunaga

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
|||| |

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
|

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
||||

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
|

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
||||

05:26.380 --> 05:28.890
|

05:36.760 --> 05:41.520
You're inept at trickery 
and unlucky to boot.

05:43.650 --> 05:47.650
Transaction with: Yuji Fukunaga

05:43.650 --> 05:47.650
Nao Kanzaki

05:43.650 --> 05:47.650
Amount

05:43.650 --> 05:47.650
Terms: Prize if Nao Kanzaki loses
 in a one-on-one card game

05:44.060 --> 05:46.790
Well, I'll be taking this now.

05:46.790 --> 05:47.660
I lost.

05:48.290 --> 05:50.990
It was my only hope, my last chance,

05:50.990 --> 05:52.870
and I still lost.

06:02.090 --> 06:04.500
Are you crying again?

06:07.330 --> 06:09.800
A-Akiyama-san!

06:11.010 --> 06:12.550
Akiyama-san...

06:12.550 --> 06:14.330
What are you doing here?

06:14.850 --> 06:19.300
All those annoying calls
 I was getting suddenly stopped.

06:19.630 --> 06:24.830
I asked the LGT Office, and lo and behold,
you'd entered the revival round.

06:25.080 --> 06:27.040
And here you are with 
that doomed expression.

06:27.040 --> 06:28.950
You're losing big-time, aren't you?

06:31.980 --> 06:34.840
Akiyama-san!

06:35.480 --> 06:36.950
Good grief.

06:38.060 --> 06:41.320
I told him everything that had happened.

06:42.440 --> 06:46.090
Even luck has turned against me.

06:46.090 --> 06:47.840
Luck, huh?

06:48.620 --> 06:52.120
You think you lost that game
 because of luck?

06:53.480 --> 06:58.220
The very notion you had a fifty-fifty 
shot at that game was BS.

06:58.220 --> 07:02.730
Fukunaga set that all up so she'd win.

07:02.730 --> 07:03.860
What?!

07:04.110 --> 07:05.860
How did she do that?

07:05.860 --> 07:07.330
Let me ask one question.

07:07.330 --> 07:09.100
Why were you assigned the face side?

07:09.530 --> 07:11.450
I chose it.

07:11.450 --> 07:14.590
She asked if I preferred
 light or darkness,

07:14.590 --> 07:16.240
and I said light.

07:16.540 --> 07:18.600
So "light" means the card's face?

07:19.010 --> 07:22.120
And if you'd picked "darkness," 
you would've gotten the reverse?

07:22.930 --> 07:24.230
Wouldn't I?

07:24.230 --> 07:25.230
No.

07:25.800 --> 07:29.850
If you'd answered darkness, she'd have 
said the joker symbolizes darkness

07:29.850 --> 07:31.600
and still assigned you the face side.

07:32.290 --> 07:36.550
The game is designed 
for the reverse side to win.

07:37.490 --> 07:39.300
What do you mean?

07:39.300 --> 07:42.430
Think back on how you played.

07:42.770 --> 07:46.560
Fukunaga mixed up the cards, 
and you drew one.

07:47.300 --> 07:52.500
At this point, the chance that you'd pull 
the double-backed card was indeed 50%.

07:53.040 --> 07:57.080
But when you drew a card face-up, 
you returned it to the bag.

07:59.370 --> 08:00.970
You see the trick now?

08:02.180 --> 08:04.760
Fukunaga's card had two backs to it.

08:04.760 --> 08:08.260
It couldn't be drawn face-up, 
so it would never be returned to the bag.

08:08.260 --> 08:10.980
But your card had a face and a back.

08:10.980 --> 08:15.080
There was a baked-in 50% chance 
of it being returned to the bag.

08:15.450 --> 08:20.840
Which means you were only getting
 the joker a third of the time.

08:15.700 --> 08:16.030
Reverse

08:16.030 --> 08:16.120
Reverse + Reverse

08:16.120 --> 08:16.970
Reverse + Reverse = Fukunaga Wins

08:16.970 --> 08:17.330
Reverse

08:17.330 --> 08:17.410
Reverse + Reverse

08:17.410 --> 08:18.430
Reverse + Reverse = Fukunaga Wins

08:18.430 --> 08:18.780
Reverse

08:18.780 --> 08:18.870
Reverse + Face

08:18.870 --> 08:19.850
Reverse + Face = Nao Wins

08:19.850 --> 08:20.040
Face

08:20.040 --> 08:21.600
Face = Restart

08:21.600 --> 08:26.290
Your chances of being the first
 to ten with one-in-three draws

08:26.290 --> 08:28.260
were a mere 6% or so.

08:28.720 --> 08:31.710
It was extremely unlikely 
that you could ever win.

08:33.800 --> 08:36.570
Why am I so stupid?

08:36.570 --> 08:39.040
Why did I agree to play 
that crooked game?

08:39.040 --> 08:41.130
Well, don't sit here crying about it.

08:41.130 --> 08:43.620
Go take back what you lost.

08:47.360 --> 08:49.370
We can't even play the games?

08:49.940 --> 08:51.370
Fukunaga-san.

08:52.710 --> 08:56.890
I want to play that game one more time.

08:57.640 --> 09:01.180
Does she realize I rigged it?

09:01.180 --> 09:05.880
No way. I could have easily 
lost the first time.

09:05.880 --> 09:07.830
I understand your reservations.

09:08.190 --> 09:12.400
So I'll give you even 
better terms than before.

09:12.690 --> 09:16.290
If I win, you only have 
to give me ten votes.

09:16.290 --> 09:19.570
But if I lose, I'll pay you 50 million.

09:21.900 --> 09:26.540
You know there's no way to 
string ten votes into a victory.

09:27.430 --> 09:33.080
But compromising is the only 
way you'll agree, isn't it?

09:33.760 --> 09:35.720
You think you can win?

09:35.720 --> 09:37.810
I don't know about that...

09:38.770 --> 09:42.040
But it's really a fifty-fifty game, right?

09:42.040 --> 09:47.590
You won the first round, 
so I figure maybe it's my time.

09:47.850 --> 09:50.580
She really can't get any dumber!

09:52.190 --> 09:54.670
Very well, but on one condition.

09:55.390 --> 09:58.250
Can we keep the rules 
of the game the same?

09:58.250 --> 09:59.110
Sure!

09:59.510 --> 10:01.990
Then I'll play.

10:02.480 --> 10:07.390
And so began the second duel 
between me and Fukunaga-san.

10:14.240 --> 10:15.800
Another one for me!

10:15.800 --> 10:18.520
Looks like I'm almost there.

10:15.800 --> 10:18.930
Nao

||||

10:15.800 --> 10:18.930
Fukunaga
|||| ||||

10:15.800 --> 10:18.930
|

10:15.800 --> 10:18.930
|

10:18.920 --> 10:21.550
And you're practically done for.

10:21.890 --> 10:24.600
This is supposed to be fifty-fifty...

10:24.600 --> 10:26.820
Why can't I seem to draw the face side?

10:28.430 --> 10:29.940
It's strange...

10:29.940 --> 10:31.690
Something's funny here.

10:31.690 --> 10:34.180
There's no funny business whatsoever.

10:34.180 --> 10:36.330
Now, let's keep the game going.

10:39.830 --> 10:40.950
I've got her!

10:42.830 --> 10:44.200
There we go!

10:45.450 --> 10:46.560
Yes!

10:48.090 --> 10:49.830
I did it!

10:50.190 --> 10:52.340
Another for me!

10:52.330 --> 10:55.340
Nao
|||| ||||

10:52.330 --> 10:55.340
|

10:52.330 --> 10:55.340
|

10:52.330 --> 10:55.340
Fukunaga
|||| ||||

10:53.770 --> 10:54.970
That's strange...

10:55.340 --> 10:57.090
Something's funny here.

10:57.630 --> 11:00.070
Are you... cheating?

11:01.770 --> 11:04.350
It's a fifty-fifty game.

11:04.350 --> 11:07.000
Why wouldn't it turn out nine to nine?

11:08.390 --> 11:10.520
If you think something's 
funny about that...

11:10.520 --> 11:12.740
Is it because you're cheating somehow?

11:13.000 --> 11:14.820
Certainly not.

11:14.820 --> 11:17.540
Now, let's move on to the final draw!

11:18.530 --> 11:21.240
Nao just got a lucky streak.

11:22.120 --> 11:25.070
I have twice the chance of winning.

11:26.610 --> 11:30.040
I'll take this last draw for sure!

11:35.270 --> 11:37.310
All right, flip it over.

11:47.060 --> 11:49.810
Yes! I win!

11:50.310 --> 11:53.150
Why? How?!

11:53.810 --> 11:56.310
Nao
|||| ||||

11:53.810 --> 11:56.310
|

11:53.810 --> 11:56.310
|

11:53.810 --> 11:56.310
|

11:53.810 --> 11:56.310
Fukunaga
|||| ||||

11:54.670 --> 11:56.310
This is weird.

11:56.310 --> 11:59.730
You did something. You cheated! I know it!

11:59.730 --> 12:01.220
What makes you say that?

12:01.220 --> 12:03.940
We both had a fifty-fifty shot.

12:03.940 --> 12:07.080
What's so weird about me winning?

12:07.570 --> 12:11.110
If you don't make good on your 
M Ticket within three minutes,

12:11.110 --> 12:14.340
I'll tell the dealer that 
you broke our contract.

12:14.340 --> 12:18.490
Though I'm afraid that will come 
with a 100-million-yen penalty.

12:20.970 --> 12:22.580
Damn it!

12:23.700 --> 12:25.270
I had won.

12:25.830 --> 12:30.230
I'd put one over on that 
sneaky Fukunaga-san!

12:31.390 --> 12:34.110
Let's give her a taste 
of her own medicine.

12:34.110 --> 12:37.020
Challenge Fukunaga to the game again.

12:37.020 --> 12:39.120
You want me to challenge her?

12:39.120 --> 12:42.050
But I only have a 6% chance of winning.

12:42.050 --> 12:44.920
That's why you're going to cheat.

12:45.450 --> 12:46.520
Cheat?!

12:46.520 --> 12:49.490
In mahjong, there's a form 
of cheating called "ganpai."

12:49.840 --> 12:53.990
You mark the backs of tiles
and use that to tell them apart.

12:55.230 --> 12:58.380
But I'm pulling cards out of a bag.

12:58.380 --> 13:00.150
I won't be able to see any markings.

13:00.150 --> 13:02.210
You'll tell by touch.

13:02.580 --> 13:06.200
For example, I've put a small 
notch in the side of this card.

13:10.500 --> 13:13.270
You're right! 
It's so obvious to the touch!

13:13.270 --> 13:14.260
Right?

13:14.260 --> 13:17.770
At the start of the game, 
you'll mark a card.

13:17.770 --> 13:21.390
Just make sure the one you mark
is the double-backed card.

13:21.390 --> 13:24.930
With that, you can pull 
whichever card you want.

13:24.930 --> 13:25.800
Right.

13:25.800 --> 13:28.530
You just need to be mindful of one thing.

13:28.900 --> 13:31.460
Be sure to pull the double-backed

13:30.580 --> 13:37.290
Nao ||||

13:30.580 --> 13:37.290
Fukunaga |||| ||

13:30.580 --> 13:37.290
|

13:31.460 --> 13:34.820
and regular cards in a ratio 
of about two to one.

13:32.950 --> 13:37.290
|

13:34.290 --> 13:37.290
|

13:34.820 --> 13:37.290
Why should I do that?

13:37.290 --> 13:41.660
Fukunaga knows the game's odds
 are rigged two to one in her favor.

13:42.050 --> 13:46.800
As long as things proceed at roughly 
that rate, she won't get suspicious.

13:46.800 --> 13:47.970
Of course!

13:47.970 --> 13:51.970
If I let Fukunaga-san take the lead, 
she won't realize anything's off.

13:52.300 --> 13:58.310
Once she gets to nine and you have five,
 begin drawing only your card.

13:58.310 --> 14:05.570
Nao
||||

13:58.310 --> 14:05.570
|

13:58.310 --> 14:05.570
|

13:58.310 --> 14:05.570
Fukunaga
|||| ||||

13:58.310 --> 14:01.170
Nine to six, nine to seven...

13:59.170 --> 14:05.570
|

14:00.580 --> 14:05.570
|

14:01.940 --> 14:05.280
Fukunaga might start to get 
suspicious at this point.

14:05.730 --> 14:08.710
But she won't be able 
to find anything amiss,

14:08.710 --> 14:13.090
because the only marked card 
will be the one inside the bag.

14:13.090 --> 14:18.070
Of course! That's why I'm marking 
the double-backed card!

14:18.080 --> 14:24.090
|||| ||

14:18.080 --> 14:24.090
|||| ||||

14:18.080 --> 14:24.090
|

14:18.080 --> 14:24.090
|

14:18.320 --> 14:20.370
Then you'll hit nine to eight.

14:18.690 --> 14:24.090
|

14:20.370 --> 14:23.670
At this point, Fukunaga will 
become deeply suspicious.

14:24.540 --> 14:27.440
But by then, it will be too late.

14:27.440 --> 14:32.170
It will be impossible for her to discover 
the trick with only two draws left.

14:32.600 --> 14:34.350
Nao
|||| ||||

14:32.600 --> 14:34.350
Fukunaga
|||| ||||

14:32.600 --> 14:34.350
|

14:32.600 --> 14:34.350
|

14:32.600 --> 14:34.350
|

14:34.690 --> 14:37.060
She fell right for our plan!

14:39.860 --> 14:43.360
I will now reveal the results 
of the sixth vote.

14:43.360 --> 14:44.360
Yuji Fukunaga


 Tetsuzo Sato

 Takayoshi Miura


 Shingo Fujita


 Hiroto Kitamura


 Kosuke Tsunoda

 Teruyuki Eda


 Takahiro Kikuzawa

 Nao Kanzaki

14:44.350 --> 14:47.440
There has also been a 
change in the vote totals,

14:44.360 --> 14:49.490
Yuji Fukunaga


 Tetsuzo Sato

 Takayoshi Miura


 Shingo Fujita

 Hiroto Kitamura

 Kosuke Tsunoda

 Teruyuki Eda


 Takahiro Kikuzawa 

 Nao Kanzaki

14:47.440 --> 14:48.700
which we will now display.

14:49.480 --> 14:51.020
A change in the votes?

14:51.020 --> 14:51.980
What's that mean?

14:52.620 --> 14:57.580
Tetsuzo Sato

14:53.010 --> 14:57.580
Takayoshi Miura

14:53.390 --> 14:57.580
Shingo Fujita

14:53.760 --> 14:57.580
Hiroto Kitamura

14:54.140 --> 14:57.580
Kosuke Tsunoda

14:54.510 --> 14:57.580
Teruyuki Eda

14:54.890 --> 14:57.580
Takahiro Kikuzawa

14:56.020 --> 14:57.580
Yuji Fukunaga

14:56.220 --> 14:57.580
Nao Kanzaki

14:57.580 --> 15:00.120
Nao Kanzaki got ten votes?!

15:00.120 --> 15:02.540
And Fukunaga's votes went down!

15:02.540 --> 15:03.460
How?

15:03.460 --> 15:06.300
The next vote will be in one hour.

15:06.300 --> 15:07.240
Until then.

15:08.620 --> 15:11.600
Hey, what's the story here, Fukunaga?!

15:16.470 --> 15:21.900
You've got your measly ten votes, 
but you still can't win.

15:25.110 --> 15:28.900
Amazing work. You really put 
one over on ol' Fukunaga.

15:28.900 --> 15:30.110
But still...

15:30.110 --> 15:32.200
Ten votes doesn't help me.

15:32.200 --> 15:33.860
Untrue.

15:33.860 --> 15:38.450
Those ten votes are how
 you carve a path forward.

15:38.870 --> 15:42.650
Tetsuzo Sato



 Takayoshi Miura


 Shingo Fujita



 Hiroto Kitamura


 Kosuke Tsunoda



Teruyuki Eda



 Takahiro Kikuzawa


 Yuji Fukunaga


 Nao Kanzaki

15:39.000 --> 15:41.870
Seventh Vote

15:41.890 --> 15:45.630
Tetsuzo Sato


 Takayoshi Miura


 Shingo Fujita


 Hiroto Kitamura


 Kosuke Tsunoda


 Teruyuki Eda


 Takahiro Kikuzawa


 Yuji Fukunaga


 Nao Kanzaki

15:42.620 --> 15:45.630
Eighth Vote

15:50.140 --> 15:51.250
Well?

15:51.250 --> 15:54.330
How does it feel to sit helplessly 
on the chopping block?

15:55.810 --> 15:59.100
I wasn't really just sitting helplessly.

16:00.320 --> 16:05.370
I had to play the doomed victim 
in front of Fukunaga-san,

16:05.370 --> 16:09.180
all while I was quietly working
 behind the scenes.

16:12.040 --> 16:13.450
Kikuzawa-san!

16:14.490 --> 16:17.520
I need to ask you a favor.

16:18.860 --> 16:20.290
Uh...

16:20.290 --> 16:23.570
We can talk about it 
after the game's over.

16:23.570 --> 16:24.290
Wait!

16:24.750 --> 16:26.550
I'll give you 70 million yen!

16:27.180 --> 16:30.430
Just hear me out. Please!

16:31.720 --> 16:36.420
As you know, I've only been 
able to earn ten votes.

16:36.420 --> 16:38.650
There's no way I can win.

16:39.020 --> 16:41.430
Yeah, that's a real shame.

16:41.430 --> 16:42.940
It's all right.

16:42.940 --> 16:46.110
I've accepted that I'm 
going to lose this game.

16:46.690 --> 16:49.930
But there's one thing I can't stand...

16:50.470 --> 16:54.600
At this rate, Fukunaga-san 
will enter the third-round game

16:54.600 --> 16:57.200
with an advantage over the rest of you.

16:57.200 --> 17:01.210
You know that she's getting 
money from all of us?

17:01.210 --> 17:04.220
Yes. And not just that.

17:04.220 --> 17:07.850
She also stole 30 million from me!

17:07.850 --> 17:09.590
Th-Thirty million?!

17:09.590 --> 17:13.130
So she's got more than 
70 million over the rest of us?

17:13.130 --> 17:14.340
That's right!

17:14.970 --> 17:21.090
She's going to have a 70-million-yen 
lead and easily sweep the third game!

17:21.480 --> 17:23.230
Yeah, that's not good.

17:23.230 --> 17:24.290
Right?

17:24.290 --> 17:28.270
For the person who deceived you all in 
the second game to come out on top...

17:28.270 --> 17:30.770
I find that deeply unjust.

17:30.770 --> 17:31.940
Yeah, agreed.

17:32.360 --> 17:33.240
So...

17:33.510 --> 17:37.740
I want to give you all of 
my remaining 70 million.

17:37.740 --> 17:38.770
What?

17:38.770 --> 17:42.850
After observing everyone over the 
course of this game, I picked you.

17:42.850 --> 17:46.700
You're the best person to beat 
Fukunaga-san in the third game!

17:46.700 --> 17:49.000
Y-You think so?

17:49.000 --> 17:51.430
You're an automotive engineer.

17:51.430 --> 17:52.880
You understand machines.

17:52.880 --> 17:55.250
I think that's a powerful weapon to have.

17:55.250 --> 18:00.170
Most of all, you had the determination 
and courage to stand up to that LGT official!

18:02.000 --> 18:06.610
The only thing Fukunaga-san 
has over you is money.

18:07.520 --> 18:12.890
So if I give you my 70 million,
 you'll be even.

18:12.890 --> 18:16.780
Then, since you're the stronger player, 
you can beat her in the third game!

18:16.780 --> 18:19.840
Yeah, I think you might be right.

18:19.840 --> 18:23.300
But... you sure you wanna 
give me 70 million?

18:23.300 --> 18:26.290
I can't give it to you 
for nothing, of course.

18:27.160 --> 18:31.040
In exchange, I want ten of your votes.

18:32.230 --> 18:34.750
Ten of my votes?

18:34.750 --> 18:37.000
Wait, I can't do that!

18:37.000 --> 18:38.020
But you can.

18:38.020 --> 18:40.530
Votes belong to the player
 who's earned them.

18:40.530 --> 18:42.680
You can buy and sell them at will.

18:42.680 --> 18:45.270
Remember the sixth vote?

18:45.590 --> 18:49.890
That was because I won a bet against 
Fukunaga-san and took ten from her.

18:45.770 --> 18:49.890
Transaction with: Nao Kanzaki

18:45.770 --> 18:49.890
Amount

18:45.770 --> 18:49.890
Yuji Fukunaga

18:45.770 --> 18:49.890
Terms: Give ten votes to Nao Kanzaki 
if she wins in a one-on-one card game.

18:50.350 --> 18:51.990
Hey, yeah.

18:51.990 --> 18:54.390
Still, why do you want ten of my votes?

18:54.390 --> 19:00.400
An official told me there are 
other revival rounds being played.

19:00.660 --> 19:02.750
It's a tournament. That makes sense.

19:02.750 --> 19:07.790
And apparently there's been a dropout,
so there's an open seat in the third game.

19:08.920 --> 19:11.050
That open seat

19:11.050 --> 19:16.100
will go to the revival round loser who 
managed to secure the most votes.

19:16.410 --> 19:18.110
Okay...

19:18.110 --> 19:22.310
The other games have already concluded,

19:22.310 --> 19:27.410
but the current loser with 
the most votes has nineteen.

19:27.680 --> 19:30.840
So if you get just ten more...

19:30.840 --> 19:33.320
I can move on to the third-round game.

19:33.750 --> 19:36.450
We all know I'm going to lose here,

19:36.450 --> 19:40.230
so it makes no difference where 
exactly the rest of you place.

19:40.230 --> 19:44.570
Which means that over half the votes 
in your total are meaningless.

19:44.860 --> 19:48.640
Isn't it only logical to sell
 some of those votes for money?

19:48.640 --> 19:51.420
And I get to use them to 
save my place in the game!

19:51.420 --> 19:53.540
Yeah, that's a win-win!

19:53.840 --> 19:55.690
And that's not all.

19:56.210 --> 20:01.820
If I make it to the third game, 
I'll do everything I can to support you.

20:01.820 --> 20:03.090
I promise I will!

20:06.690 --> 20:12.440
I can enter the third game with money 
 to rival Fukunaga and an alliance?

20:13.270 --> 20:14.400
What do you think?

20:16.520 --> 20:19.300
I'm down! Sounds great to me!

20:19.690 --> 20:21.540
Thank you so much!

20:22.510 --> 20:26.460
We added a number of 
clauses to the contract.

20:27.000 --> 20:31.660
The payout of the 70 million would only 
take place after the votes were moved.

20:31.660 --> 20:35.280
In other words, 
after the tally of the ninth vote

20:34.790 --> 20:46.220
Transaction with: Nao Kanzaki

20:34.790 --> 20:46.220
Terms: To be paid after ten of my votes have been moved
 to Nao Kanzaki after the ninth vote. Payment may be made
 anytime before the tenth vote. This agreement may not be 
spoken of with any other players.

20:34.790 --> 20:46.220
Amount

20:35.280 --> 20:37.900
and before the tenth vote began.

20:38.420 --> 20:44.260
And we agreed not to discuss the nature 
of our agreement with anyone else.

20:44.260 --> 20:46.210
Okay, that'll do it!

20:46.210 --> 20:50.690
All right, let's work together in the 
third game and take down Fukunaga!

20:50.690 --> 20:51.670
Yeah!

20:53.820 --> 20:56.780
Then came the ninth vote.

20:58.730 --> 21:01.730
I will now reveal the results of the vote.

21:01.730 --> 21:03.740
Tetsuzo Sato


 Takayoshi Miura


 Shingo Fujita

Takahiro Kikuzawa


 Kosuke Tsunoda

 Teruyuki Eda


 Hiroto Kitamura

 Yuji Fukunaga

 Nao Kanzaki

21:04.540 --> 21:07.740
Next, I will reveal the movement of votes.

21:08.100 --> 21:09.130
Here we go!

21:10.310 --> 21:15.150
I'm going to lose ten votes, 
and Kanzaki will gain ten.

21:19.790 --> 21:20.920
What the...

21:23.510 --> 21:25.510
Nao Kanzaki


 Tetsuzo Sato

 Takayoshi Miura


 Shingo Fujita

 Takahiro Kikuzawa


 Kosuke Tsunoda

 Teruyuki Eda


 Hiroto Kitamura

 Yuji Fukunaga

21:24.390 --> 21:25.500
Wha...

21:25.500 --> 21:28.150
What the hell?!

21:28.150 --> 21:31.350
Hey! What's going on here?!

21:31.910 --> 21:33.010
What do you mean?

21:33.650 --> 21:36.970
I got ten votes from each of you!

21:37.280 --> 21:40.370
Ten votes... from each of us?

21:42.020 --> 21:44.350
You tricked me!

21:44.350 --> 21:46.030
And what's wrong with that?

21:47.740 --> 21:50.080
This is the Liar Game.

21:50.080 --> 21:52.440
It's all about tricking people.

21:53.500 --> 21:54.580
Right?

21:54.580 --> 21:56.300
A-Akiyama?!

21:56.300 --> 21:58.030
When did you...

21:58.030 --> 21:59.900
You'd better come to terms with it.

21:59.900 --> 22:02.380
The paradigm just shifted.

22:03.180 --> 22:06.640
The person in full control 
of this game is Nao Kanzaki.

22:07.910 --> 22:13.110
Your lives are completely in our hands.

23:45.060 --> 23:50.060
Next Time

23:45.060 --> 23:50.060
Salvation
